<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Batu Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1205–1255</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Batu Khan was a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> and founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. He led the Mongol western campaign of 1236 to 1242, conquering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> and the principalities of Rus' and invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. He then ruled the western steppe from his capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> on the Volga, and Russian princes such as Daniel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> had to submit to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/batu-khan.jpg" alt="Batu Khan" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rashid al-Din Hamadani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batu_Khan_on_the_Throne_by_Rashid_al-Din_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Batu was a son of Jochi and so officially a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, although Jochi's paternity is highly uncertain. After Jochi died, Genghis Khan divided his lands between Batu and his older brother Orda and installed Batu as khan. Orda's White Horde ruled the lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and Lake Balkhash, while Batu's Golden Horde ruled the lands west of the Volga. According to Giovanni da Pian del Carpine, Batu was kind enough to his own people but greatly feared by them, and most cruel in fight.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a> after the war against the Jin, the Great Khan Ögedei ordered Batu to conquer western nations. In 1235 he was given an army of possibly 130,000, which was actually commanded by the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a>. It crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and invaded Volga Bulgaria in 1236. In November 1237 Batu demanded the allegiance of Yuri II of Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a>. When Yuri refused, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a>, which was destroyed after six days and never restored to its former glory. They took Vladimir after a siege that began on 4 February 1238 and burned it, and on 4 March destroyed the army Yuri had raised on the Sit' River. Kiev was stormed in December 1240, and the Rus' principalities became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> then invaded central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in three groups. One devastated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-the-pious">Henry II the Pious</a>, Duke of Silesia, and the Grand Master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a>. Batu's own army defeated King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iv-of-hungary">Béla IV of Hungary</a> and his allies at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohi">Battle of Mohi</a> on 11 April. Plans to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> were under way when news came of Ögedei's death in December 1241, and the Mongols withdrew in late spring 1242 as the princes were recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Batu made his camps on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and, as one of the oldest of the descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, became viceroy over the western parts of the empire. He controlled routine affairs among the Rus' princes, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-ii-of-vladimir">Yaroslav II of Vladimir</a>, whom he confirmed as suzerain, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-of-chernigov">Michael of Chernigov</a> was put to death for refusing to bow before the tablets of Genghis Khan. Danylo of Halych submitted to him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a>. Batu delayed the election of Güyük, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> in 1246, and in 1250 he called a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a> on his own territory that offered the throne to him; he nominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a> instead, who was proclaimed Great Khan in 1251.</p><p>Batu died in 1255 and left affairs of state to his son Sartaq. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berke">Berke</a> inherited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> after a brief regency. The Golden Horde ruled the steppes of what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, and Batu's line held the throne until 1360.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Batu Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jochi died around 1225–1227, before his father, and his son <b>Batu</b> inherited leadership of the western wing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1237 <b>Batu Khan</b>'s armies returned, destroying Ryazan and Vladimir in 1237–1238 and Chernigov and Pereyaslavl in 1239, and in December 1240 they stormed Kiev.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mongol invasion exposed Hungary's weakness: on 11 April 1241 <b>Batu</b>'s army destroyed the royal host at the Battle of Mohi on the Sajó river.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Batu</b>’s army took Ryazan in December 1237, stormed Vladimir in February 1238, killing the grand prince’s family in the burning Dormition Cathedral, and destroyed Grand Prince Yuri II’s army at the Battle of the Sit River on 4 March 1238.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The decision of the great khan Ögedei to launch a western campaign under <b>Batu</b> and Subutai sealed its fate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After a short siege, the Mongol army of <b>Batu Khan</b> destroyed Ryazan, the first city of Kievan Rus' to be besieged in the invasion.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-21">21 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. Died in Sarai. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46,105&amp;year=1237" aria-label="Mongolia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7032 544 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Batu Khan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7482 814h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7482 814h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46,105&amp;year=1237">What was here in 1237? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
